From: Francesco Riosa <me@francesco-riosa.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 20:21:46 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] =?UTF-8?q?fix(parser):=20don't=20let=20a=20nested=20${?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=E2=80=A6}/$(=E2=80=A6)=20on=20a=20here-tag=20line=20steal=20th?=
 =?UTF-8?q?e=20here-doc's=20tokens?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A `${…}`, `$(…)`, `$((…))`, or `$[…]` on the same line as a here tag --
typically inside a redirection target that follows the tag -- was
tokenized while the *outer* here-document was still mid-collection.
`delimit_current_token`'s `CurrentTokenIsHereTag` / `NextLineIsHereDoc`
arms then captured the construct's sub-tokens into the here tag's
`pending_tokens_after`, corrupting the enclosing word: `cat <<EOF >
"${base}.c"` produced a redirect to a file literally named `base` plus a
junk word `"${}.c"`, and `cat <<${VAR}` registered `VAR` (not `${VAR}`)
as the delimiter. Both are execution bugs, not just serialization.

Give a nested construct a fresh here-document context for the duration of
its own scan -- the way bash's `parse_comsub` recurses with its own
here-doc list. `suspend_outer_here_docs` / `restore_outer_here_docs`
bracket `consume_nested_construct` and a new
`consume_braced_parameter_expansion` (extracted verbatim from the inline
`${…}` loop). A here-document opened *inside* the construct still works
-- it sees the empty suspended context and is consumed before the
terminator; anything left open is merged back so bash's "unterminated
here-document in command substitution" quirk still reads its trailing
body.

Adds two snapshot tests covering `cat <<EOF > "${base}.c"` /
`... "out$((1 + 2)).c" 2>&1` and `cat <<${VAR}`.
